Hospital Management System

Introduction

The Hospital Management System is a C++ Object-Oriented Programming project designed to manage hospital staff, patients, appointments, and admitted patients.

The system focuses on applying important Object-Oriented Programming concepts to a real-world hospital environment. It provides features such as patient registration, doctor management, appointment scheduling, doctor assignment, patient admission, validation, and file-based data storage.

Why This Project?

Managing patient and doctor information manually can be difficult and time-consuming. This project provides a simple computerized system for organizing important hospital records.

The main purpose of this project is to understand how C++ OOP concepts can be applied to build a practical real-world management system.

Main Features

- Add and view doctors/staff
- Register new patients
- Generate patient IDs and appointment numbers
- Validate patient information
- Validate CNIC and phone numbers
- Schedule appointments using date, day, and timing
- Automatically assign doctors according to:
  - Doctor gender
  - Duty day
  - Duty timing
- Admit patients
- Store admission and discharge dates
- Prevent duplicate patient CNICs
- Save patient data in files
- Load previously saved patient data
- Maintain patient history
- Console-based colored interface

Classes

Person

"Person" is the base class that contains common personal information such as:

- Name
- Age
- CNIC
- Phone number
- Gender

It also provides validation and input/display functionality.

Staff

"Staff" inherits from "Person" and represents hospital doctors/staff.

It contains information such as:

- Staff ID
- Specialization
- Duty days
- Duty timing

The current system uses Cardiologist as the fixed specialization.

Patient

"Patient" inherits from "Person" and manages patient-related information.

It includes:

- Patient ID
- Appointment number
- Appointment date
- Day
- Timing
- Doctor gender preference
- Assigned doctor
- Bill status
- Disease

AdmittedPatient

"AdmittedPatient" inherits from "Patient" and represents patients who are admitted to the hospital.

It additionally stores:

- Admission date
- Discharge date

Hospital

"Hospital" is the main management class. It maintains collections of doctors, patients, and admitted patients.

It is responsible for:

- Adding doctors
- Viewing doctors
- Registering patients
- Admitting patients
- Assigning doctors
- Checking duplicate CNICs
- Saving data
- Loading data

OOP Concepts Used

This project demonstrates several important C++ OOP concepts:

Inheritance

"Staff" and "Patient" inherit common personal information from "Person".

"AdmittedPatient" further inherits from "Patient".

Encapsulation

Data members are kept inside classes and accessed through appropriate member functions.

Polymorphism

Virtual functions such as patient and person input/display functions demonstrate runtime polymorphism through inheritance.

Abstraction

Different classes represent specific real-world entities and hide their internal implementation details.

Pointers and References

The project uses C++ references in functions where objects are processed without unnecessary copying.

Data Validation

The system validates important user inputs before accepting them.

Examples include:

- Name must contain alphabets and spaces
- Age must be within a valid range
- CNIC must contain exactly 13 digits
- Phone number must contain exactly 11 digits
- Appointment date is processed to determine the day

Doctor Assignment System

One of the main features of the project is automatic doctor assignment.

When a patient creates an appointment, the system checks the available doctors according to:

1. Patient's selected doctor gender
2. Appointment day
3. Appointment timing

If a matching doctor is found, the doctor is automatically assigned to the patient. Otherwise, the system reports that a doctor is not available.

File Handling

The project uses file handling to preserve important information even after the program closes.

The system works with files for:

- Patient records
- Admitted patient records
- Patient history

This allows previously stored information to be loaded again when required.

Technologies Used

- Language: C++
- Programming Paradigm: Object-Oriented Programming
- Libraries: iostream, fstream, vector, string, cctype, ctime
- Platform-specific libraries: Windows.h, conio.h, cstdio
- Data Storage: Text files
- Design: UML-based class design

Project Structure

The main components of the system are:

"Person"
↓
"Staff"

"Person"
↓
"Patient"
↓
"AdmittedPatient"

"Hospital" manages:

- Doctors
- Patients
- Admitted Patients

Future Improvements

Possible future improvements could include:

- A graphical user interface
- More hospital departments and specializations
- Improved date validation
- Search and update functionality
- More detailed billing management
- Database integration

Conclusion

This project demonstrates how C++ Object-Oriented Programming can be used to design a practical Hospital Management System.

Through this project, concepts such as inheritance, encapsulation, polymorphism, abstraction, file handling, validation, vectors, and class relationships are applied together in a real-world scenario.

The project is designed not only to perform basic hospital management tasks but also to strengthen practical programming, system-design, and problem-solving skills.